# Biome lint rule patterns for implementing `NoMisplacedAssertion`

Repo root is `/workspace/repo`. **Critically: four of the five files you asked about are codegen-generated** (they carry `//! Generated file, do not edit by hand, see xtask/codegen` headers). The intended workflow is to run the scaffolder, then edit only the rule file:

```
cargo run -p xtask_codegen -- new-lintrule --kind=js --name=noMisplacedAssertion
```
(from `/workspace/repo/justfile:67`). This generates the rule stub and regenerates `nursery.rs`, `options.rs`, `rules.rs`, and inserts the category. You then fill in the rule logic and run codegen again for docs/registries. If you edit by hand, you must keep all five files in sync exactly as shown below.

---

## 1. Reference rule: call-expression + semantic-model + import/binding resolution

**`/workspace/repo/crates/biome_js_analyze/src/lint/nursery/no_console.rs`** (full file, lines 1-88) is the best match — it queries `JsCallExpression` through the semantic model and resolves bindings against imports. Key structure:

Imports (lines 1-10):
```rust
use crate::{services::semantic::Semantic, JsRuleAction};
use biome_analyze::{
    context::RuleContext, declare_rule, ActionCategory, FixKind, Rule, RuleDiagnostic, RuleSource,
};
use biome_console::markup;
use biome_diagnostics::Applicability;
use biome_js_syntax::{
    global_identifier, AnyJsMemberExpression, JsCallExpression, JsExpressionStatement,
};
use biome_rowan::{AstNode, BatchMutationExt};
```

`declare_rule!` macro (lines 12-30) — the doc comment becomes the rule docs; `expect_diagnostic` fenced blocks are used for snapshot tests:
```rust
declare_rule! {
    /// Disallow the use of `console`.
    /// ...
    /// ```js,expect_diagnostic
    /// console.error('hello world')
    /// ```
    pub NoConsole {
        version: "1.6.0",
        name: "noConsole",
        source: RuleSource::Eslint("no-console"),
        recommended: false,
        fix_kind: FixKind::Unsafe,
    }
}
```

`Rule` trait impl (lines 32-49) — note `type Query = Semantic<JsCallExpression>` (gives `ctx.model()` for binding resolution) and the `model.binding(&reference).is_none()` pattern for detecting a global vs. an imported/declared name:
```rust
impl Rule for NoConsole {
    type Query = Semantic<JsCallExpression>;
    type State = ();
    type Signals = Option<Self::State>;
    type Options = ();

    fn run(ctx: &RuleContext<Self>) -> Self::Signals {
        let call_expression = ctx.query();
        let model = ctx.model();
        let callee = call_expression.callee().ok()?;
        let member_expression = AnyJsMemberExpression::cast_ref(callee.syntax())?;
        let object = member_expression.object().ok()?;
        let (reference, name) = global_identifier(&object)?;
        if name.text() != "console" {
            return None;
        }
        model.binding(&reference).is_none().then_some(())
    }
```

`diagnostic` uses `rule_category!()` (a macro auto-available inside `declare_rule!` scope), a range, and `markup!` with `.note(...)` (lines 51-66). `action` builds a `JsRuleAction` via `ctx.root().begin()` mutation (lines 68-87).

Two other useful references I read:
- **`no_focused_tests.rs`** (lines 1-153) — `type Query = Ast<JsCallExpression>` (no semantic model), uses `node.is_test_call_expression()`, `callee.contains_a_test_pattern()`, `callee.get_callee_member_name()` helpers — directly relevant for detecting test-call context, which `NoMisplacedAssertion` likely needs.
- **`no_nodejs_modules.rs`** (lines 1-65) — simplest import-inspecting rule, `type Query = Ast<AnyJsImportSpecifierLike>`, `type State = TextRange`, no `action`.

